  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 AM EST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nFriday & Saturday...\n\nCurrent clear skies and temps in the mid to upper 30s will continue \nthrough sunrise. Expected highs today are right around the 70 degree \nmark, with light easterly winds as the surface high pressure hangs \nout over West Virginia. A few upper-level clouds may meander their \nway through the area today, and may linger in the Lake Cumberland \narea throughout this afternoon. Friday night will once again bring \nlows in the mid to upper 30s, clear skies, and light easterly winds \nthat will work to become southerly as the high pressure pushes off \nto the northeast. \n\nAhead of Sunday's cold front, Saturday will have continue the warm \ntemps in the low 70s and light southerly winds. Clear skies will \nprevail, and low RH values will be present. Specifically, east \ncentral Kentucky and the Lake Cumberland region will likely see RH \nvalues in the low to mid 20s, potentially impactful for fire \nweather. Overnight, cloud cover will increase ahead of the shortwave \ntrough. Scattered showers are unlikely ahead of the front, as the \ndry column of air will likely evaporate most precip before it hits \nthe ground. Overnight lows will be near 50 in Southern Indiana and \ndecrease from W to E, with lows around 40 in East Central KY and \nbelow 40 in valleys.\n\n\nSunday - Thanksgiving Night...\n\nPre-frontal high temps on Sunday will be in the upper 60s, as cloud \ncover will prevent bulk diurnal heating as in days prior. Winds will \nbe 5-10 kts out of the south. As the front passes late Sunday \nevening, the bulk of the showers will accompany it. However, that \nestablished dry air mass will continue to evaporate showers, so \nexpected rainfall values have decreased to approximately 0.10\" for \nSouthern Indiana and a few hundredths east of I-65. \n\nFollowing the cold front, overnight lows will range from the upper \n30s in Southern Indiana and Central Kentucky to the mid-40s in the \nBluegrass and South Central KY. Expect highs in the mid-50s on \nMonday, with northerly winds at 5-10 knots and clouds clearing out \nafter sunrise. RH values will recover to the mid-40s during the \nexpected daytime low period.\n\nTuesday through Thursday brings highs in the upper 40s and low 50s \nwith overnight lows in the upper 20s. Light northerly to \nnortheasterly winds will prevail, although Tuesday afternoon may see \nsome gusts in the 15+ kt range due to the pressure differential from \nN to S as a High swings through the Great Lakes. Scattered clouds on \nTuesday will turn into denser coverage on Wednesday and Thursday \nahead of the next potential system. \n\nAt the moment, Wednesday afternoon appears to push a plume of \ntropical moisture northward through Alabama and Tennessee. What is \ncurrently known of this plume is that it appears to be weak, showery \nprecipitation as PWATs are under an inch. Many models are attempting \nto resolve precipitation in a mixed format. However, agreement in \nthe precipitation type, placement, timing, and quantity is extremely \npoor as the strength of the associated Low has yet to be established \nin a reliable manner. It would be unfavorable to assign a specific \nprecipitation type to this system at this particular moment, as \nmodels are disagreeing with each other and, more importantly, \nthemselves in between runs. Additionally, ground temperatures should \nbe more than warm enough to melt traces of frozen precip should it \nfall.\n\nWednesday's temperature profile is another reason that precipitation \ntype cannot currently be assigned; surface low temperatures are \nexpected to be in the upper 20s and low 30s with light northerly \nwinds. Thanksgiving's highs are in the upper 40s, with light \nnortherly winds and cloud cover clearing out from W to E as the \nGulf Low pushes further northeast.",
